## Overview

The answer assistant is a multi-turn Q&A. The reader asks why the first CoreBox search is slow, and one TxStreamElement writes the answer: a heading, a paragraph with numbered citations, a list and a code block come in word by word on one cadence. Once it is done, the template asks a follow-up for the reader, "How much memory does warming up take?", and that answer hands its table to TxStreamMarkdown, which writes it row by row.

It fits answer engines, help centres and in-product AI assistants: anywhere a question gets an answer whose claims have to be traceable. Every answer in the template is canned text, and the table is sample data for a qualitative comparison only.

- It starts once the template scrolls into view, and the first answer pauses once on the way. About 1.6 seconds after it is done, the template asks one follow-up. A click or any key inside the template stops that and hands the conversation to you.
- The action row (copy, regenerate, thumbs up and down), the sources and the follow-ups appear only once an answer is done. Picking a follow-up adds a new turn below; a question typed into the input gets a canned answer.
- While an answer is being written, Stop generating ends the source at once. What has arrived is still revealed, the answer is marked Generation stopped, and it can be regenerated.
- Clicking a numbered citation opens that turn's source list and shows "The host would open …" above the input; the page never navigates.
- The conversation follows the newest content and stops following while you scroll up; the page itself never moves. In the docs column (640–959px) answers take the full column, and from 960px they are centred. Narrow (< 640px) hides the header's status and the answers' avatar. With reduced motion both turns render complete, and no timer starts.

## Customization

- Wire a real service by appending the model's tokens to `content` and keeping `streaming` true until the source has finished. Stopping is setting `streaming` to false: the element reveals what has arrived.
- Citations are numbered in the order of `sources`: `[n]` resolves to `sources[n - 1]`, and a marker without a source stays text. Copy and share the full text you hold, never what is on screen, which trails the source by up to `maxLagMs`.
- Row ids in the conversation must be globally unique. The template takes a new session prefix on every reset and uses it as TxConversationStream's `key`, so measured row heights never carry over into a new conversation.
- New rows go in front of a permanent last row (the tail). TxConversationStream keeps its last item outside the virtual list and remounts it when a new one arrives; with the tail there, each turn mounts once and an answer being written is never interrupted.

